import "core"


# DISABLED — a compiler gap, kept as the repro of it.
  An [if] whose two branches produce different values derives an overlap-agreement test that pairs
  those branches against each other and reports that they disagree. Branching is the whole of what
  an [if] is for, so what the test asserts is that the [if] does not do what it was written to do.
  The failure below is that test:
    FAIL: /greet.$db8ace :: overlap-agreement
    Generated TupleVal /greet.$db8ace(/formal = TupleVal /Data/Primitive/True,
                                      /$db8ace-arg = TupleVal /$M, /name = "")
    Failed to build arrow tree from
      /greet.$db8ace(/name=/Data/String, /$db8ace-arg=/$M, /formal=/Data/Primitive/True)
      to /Data/Primitive/Integer
  Two things are wrong and the first is the cause.
  @/$db8ace-arg = TupleVal /$M@ is the argument that says which branch this is. The [if] declaration
  preprocessor (@Syntax/Ct/Desugarf/Preprocessors.hs@, @ifDeclPreprocessor@) rewrites the arm into a
  generated function of one argument — @/Catln/ThenElse/fromBool(v= <the condition>)@ — with one arm
  per branch, each written as a hole aliased to [/Catln/Then] or [/Catln/Else]. Those two arms ought
  to have disjoint domains, since a [/Catln/ThenElse] is one member or the other and never both.
  They do not: the argument arrives as a metavariable, so @deriveOverlapTests@ intersects the two
  domains, gets something that is not bottom, and pairs the branches as overlapping definitions that
  must agree.
  The second is a consequence of the generated function having no declaration at all. The
  destination each clause is built toward is the function's declared return type, and
  @Eval/Testing.hs@ falls back to @intType@ where there is none — so the failure is reported as the
  branch not reaching @/Data/Primitive/Integer@ whatever the branch actually returns. That fallback
  is only ever reached by a generated function, and is why an [if] returning an [Integer] is the one
  that has always worked: @test/Integration/code/ifThenElse.ct@ passes, and passes for two reasons
  at once, since its branches also agree on the input the generator produces.
  Guards do not have this gap. @greet(name, formal) | formal= …@ beside @greet(name, formal) else =@
  writes the same choice as arms of the declared function, and a guarded arm is excluded from
  overlap pairing because a clause chosen by a condition is not one chosen by its domain. That is
  what @stack/rss/main.ct@ uses, and why.
greet(name -> String, formal -> Boolean) -> String= if(formal)
  Then= "Dear " ++ name

  Else= "Hi " ++ name

main{io -> IO}= io.exit(val= 0)
